From d8ce4e59b095fcdcbce567b16682f298267f0484 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 10:17:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Frame packing on nvidia-drm drawn by the GPU
 into block-linear buffers

The owner's RTX 4080 SUPER (620.73.04, open modules) with 1.2.3 in a
1080p24 frame-packed mode: NVIDIA's GBM made no linear buffer of
2048x2205 for scanout and rendering ("Invalid argument"), and GL could
not draw into the driver's dumb buffers imported into EGL ("storage
error 0x502, framebuffer status 0x8cd6"), so each frame was read back
and copied by the processor: 40.45 ms a frame on average, 45.48 ms at
most, where a frame of 23.976 Hz lasts 41.7 ms.

NVIDIA's EGL draws into no buffer whose lines lie one after another
that its display engine can scan out; what it draws into and scans out
is block linear. nvidia-drm with Hades' patch
(nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9087) takes a frame-packed frame from an
uncompressed block-linear framebuffer too, its right eye from the first
block row at or after vtotal that lies a multiple of 1024 bytes into
it. So the first buffers that Kodi makes for such a mode, where the
driver refuses the window's, are now block-linear buffers of GBM
(COwnScanout::Memory::GBM_EYES): with the plane's block-linear
modifiers only, BlockLinearBufferHeight() lines high, the right eye's
line computed from the modifier and pitch of what GBM made
(BlockLinearRightEyeRow(), the kernel's rule). Kodi draws the frame as
before into the framebuffer object of the frame, and the GPU puts each
eye, flipped, where the buffer holds it (EyesBlit(): two blits, each
value copied as it is). Nothing is read back. Before use each buffer is
probed (EyesProbe(): the first and last line of each eye, of values that
differ from line to line, read back from the rows the display engine
reads, the lines next to them black) and asked in a test commit of the
mode, as the others are. Where they cannot be made or are refused, the
linear buffers and the dumb ones follow as before.

The copy of the processor, where it stays, is faster and the same
bytes: the frame is read back through pixel buffers in eight bands,
each with a fence, so that the processor writes one band while the GPU
sends the next; the lines are written into the write-combined mapping
of the dumb buffer with 16-byte streaming stores, never read, red and
blue exchanged four pixels at a time where they must be; a dumb buffer
is made black the same way. Where a step of the pixel buffers fails,
the frame is read back at once as before. kodi.log says after 240
frames, and every 2400 frames after, what a frame costs: waiting for
the GPU, writing, and at most. Where GL cannot draw into a dumb buffer,
kodi.log also says what EGL lists DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR of the format
as (external only, or drawn into).

Check this setup > 3D: frame packing asks for the block-linear buffers
first as a video would ("eyes", "eyeswhy", "buffers": "blocklinear",
"draw": "gpu"), and where the dumb buffers are copied into, what the
copy of a frame costs here ("copyms", ten black frames into a buffer
that never goes out).

2D, Dolby Vision and the buffers of more than 8 bits are not touched:
everything here is of a frame-packed mode of nvidia-drm.
